## Why This Is Asked
The Trie with prefix count mechanism addresses dynamic dictionary requirements in real-time applications like chat search, testing understanding of per-node frequency tracking, deletion correctness, and memory efficiency trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with passCount and endCount for frequency tracking
- O(length) time complexity for all operations
- Memory management through node pruning
- Correct handling of duplicates and edge cases
- Amortized deletion: pruning happens during erase, no separate cleanup needed

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.pass = 0;
    this.end = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    node.pass++;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) {
        node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      node.pass++;
    }
    node.end++;
  }

  exists(word) {
    const node = this._findNode(word);
    return node !== null && node.end > 0;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    const node = this._findNode(prefix);
    return node ? node.pass : 0;
  }

  erase(word) {
    if (!this.exists(word)) return false;

    let node = this.root;
    node.pass--;

    for (const ch of word) {
      const child = node.children.get(ch);
      child.pass--;
      if (child.pass === 0) {
        node.children.delete(ch);
        node = null;
        break;
      }
      node = child;
    }

    if (node) node.end--;
    return true;
  }

  _findNode(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return null;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node;
  }
}

// Usage
const trie = new Trie();
trie.insert('apple');
trie.insert('apple');
trie.insert('app');
trie.exists('apple');        // true
trie.countPrefix('app');      // 3
trie.erase('apple');
trie.exists('apple');        // true (still one left)
trie.countPrefix('app');     // 2
trie.erase('apple');
trie.exists('apple');        // false
trie.countPrefix('app');      // 1
trie.erase('nonexistent');   // false (safe)
```

## Memory Management
Nodes are allocated only when needed and automatically pruned when `pass` reaches zero during erase, ensuring no memory leaks.

## Duplicate Handling
Since `end` tracks word frequency, duplicate inserts increment the counter; duplicate erases decrement it until the count reaches zero.

## Edge Cases
- Erase on non-existent word: returns false, no changes
- Erase on word with duplicate: only decrements count, preserves other instances
- Empty string handling: root node's counters track this case

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to blend Trie prefix search with balanced BSTs for dynamic scores, plus a top-k retrieval strategy that scales efficiently. It tests practical update propagation and memory trade-offs in a high-throughput autocomplete system.

## Key Concepts
- Trie for prefix lookup
- AVL/balanced BST per node for phrase-score storage
- Top-k extraction via a front-to-back heap or subtree aggregation
- Update propagation costs and memory considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.words = new AVLTree(); // key: phrase, value: score
    this.topK = new MaxHeap(k); // top-k scores for subtree
  }
}

class AutoCompleteDictionary {
  constructor(k = 10) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.k = k;
  }

  insertPhrase(phrase, score) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of phrase) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) {
        node.next.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.next.get(char);
      node.words.insert(phrase, score);
      node.topK.insert(score);
    }
  }

  queryTopK(prefix, k) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTopK(k);
  }
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis
- insertPhrase: O(L log n) where L is phrase length, n is phrases per prefix
- updateScore: O(L log n) for BST updates plus O(L) for heap propagation
- deletePhrase: O(L log n) for BST removal and heap cleanup
- queryTopK: O(L + k log k) for traversal plus heap extraction

## Memory Trade-offs
- Trie structure: O(N × L) where N is total phrases, L is average length
- BST per node: O(N) total across all nodes
- Top-k heaps: O(N × k) worst-case, but typically O(N) with pruning

## Edge Cases
- Duplicate phrases: update existing entry instead of inserting
- Score ties: use lexicographic ordering as tiebreaker
- Empty prefix: return global top-k from root node
- Large k: consider segment tree for better range queries

## Comparison with Alternatives
- Hash map + sorting: O(1) insert but O(n log n) prefix queries
- Suffix tree: better substring search but higher memory overhead
- Pure Trie with scores: O(L) queries but O(n) updates for score changes
- Hybrid approach (proposed): balances prefix efficiency with dynamic updates

## Why This Is Asked

The question tests ability to derive a linear-time, in-place solution for a data integrity problem common in interview tasks; relies on cyclic sort to exploit value-domain mapping, and requires careful handling of duplicates and missing numbers.

